useCasesLede: "Every one of these is a drafting or comprehension task with a lawyer's review at the end. None of them is legal judgement, and the program is explicit about that boundary."
useCases:
  - title: "First drafts from precedent and matter facts"
    body: "A starting document assembled from your own precedent and the facts of the matter, so the practitioner is editing and exercising judgement rather than retyping. Your precedents stay the source."
  - title: "Chronologies from a bundle"
    body: "A dated sequence of events extracted from correspondence and documents, with sources noted, as a working draft for the practitioner to verify. The reading still has to happen — it happens faster and in the right order."
  - title: "Summarising long documents"
    body: "Contracts, reports and correspondence chains reduced to the terms, obligations and dates that matter, so a matter can be picked up quickly without missing a clause."
  - title: "Client update letters"
    body: "Plain-English updates drafted from the file — what has happened, what it means, what happens next — so clients are informed without a partner losing an hour to it."
  - title: "Attendance and file notes"
    body: "Rough notes from a conference turned into a structured file note on the day, for the practitioner to check and sign, rather than reconstructed a week later."
  - title: "Explaining the law to a client"
    body: "A judgment, a legislative change or a clause turned into something a commercial client can make a decision from, with the practitioner confirming accuracy before it is sent."
concern:
  heading: "Fabricated citations, and confidentiality"
  body: >
    <p>We'll start with the failure everyone in the profession has now read about.
    General-purpose AI tools will invent case citations that look entirely real —
    correct format, plausible names, confident tone — and practitioners in several
    jurisdictions have been sanctioned for filing them. This is not a rare glitch; it is
    a predictable property of how the tools work, and any training that glosses over it
    is dangerous. We teach it as a core module: never cite what you have not opened,
    treat every authority as unverified until checked in a real database, and understand
    why the output is confident regardless of whether it is true.</p>
    <p>On confidentiality: client information is subject to privilege and to your
    professional conduct rules. The program covers which tools have terms that make
    business use defensible, what must never be pasted into a consumer tier, and how to
    build workflows where privileged material stays inside systems you control.</p>
    <p>This is training in using the tools, not legal advice on your obligations. Your
    practice remains responsible for compliance with its conduct rules, and we'd expect
    your principal to sign off the workflow before it is used on live matters.</p>
faq:
  - q: "Isn't AI too risky for legal work?"
    a: "Untrained use is genuinely risky, and that risk is already in your firm — staff are using these tools whether or not there is a policy. Trained use, with verification built in and the boundaries understood, is considerably safer than the unsupervised use happening now. That gap is the entire argument for training."
  - q: "Can it do legal research?"
    a: "Not reliably, and we teach people not to rely on it for that. General-purpose models do not have authoritative access to Australian case law and will fabricate confidently. Research belongs in a proper legal database. Where AI helps is once you have the material — summarising, structuring, drafting and explaining."
  - q: "Will this help support staff as well as solicitors?"
    a: "Often more. Legal secretaries, paralegals and practice managers do a large share of the drafting, formatting and correspondence, and that is the work that compresses most. Many firms start there and bring practitioners in after."
  - q: "We bill by the hour. Doesn't this reduce revenue?"
    a: "It's a fair question and it depends on where your constraint is. If you are capacity-constrained and turning work away, this converts unbillable drafting time into billable matter time. If you are demand-constrained, the saving lands as margin. Firms on fixed-fee or capped work see it most directly."
  - q: "Do we need a firm-wide AI policy first?"
    a: "You need one, and you probably don't have one yet. Writing it is part of the program rather than a prerequisite — teams draft their own during the course, informed by how the tools actually behave rather than by guesswork."
